      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line, we start by determining the direction vector of the line. The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the two points on the line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5-2, 1-2, -2-1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\n\\[\nx = 2 + 3t, \\quad y = 2 - t, \\quad z = 1 - 3t\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point is 4. We need to find the corresponding value of \\( t \\). Setting \\( x = 4 \\) in the parametric equation for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t \\implies 2 = 3t \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the parametric equation for \\( z \\):\n\n\\[\nz = 1 - 3t = 1 - 3 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
