      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line that passes through the points \\((2, 2, 1)\\) and \\((5, 1, -2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we need to determine the parametric equations of the line and then use the given \\( x \\)-coordinate to find the corresponding \\( z \\)-coordinate.\n\n### Step 1: Find the direction vector of the line\nFirst, we find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line. The direction vector can be obtained by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Write the parametric equations of the line\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n(x, y, z) = (2, 2, 1) + t(3, -1, -3)\n\\]\nwhere \\( t \\) is a parameter.\n\n### Step 3: Substitute the \\( x \\)-coordinate into the parametric equation\nWe are given that the \\( x \\)-coordinate of the point is 4. Therefore, we set \\( x = 4 \\) in the parametric equation:\n\\[\n4 = 2 + t \\cdot 3\n\\]\nSolving for \\( t \\):\n\\[\n4 = 2 + 3t\n\\]\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 3t\n\\]\n\\[\n2 = 3t\n\\]\n\\[\nt = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Find the corresponding \\( z \\)-coordinate\nNow, we substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the \\( z \\)-coordinate equation:\n\\[\nz = 1 + t \\cdot (-3)\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 + \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot (-3)\n\\]\n\\[\nz = 1 - 2\n\\]\n\\[\nz = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
